# Solution — GIAM 5.3 Problem 5

## Problem

Give an inductive proof that $ \forall n\in\mathbb{N} $, $ 6\mid3n^{2}+3n-12 $.

## Answer

$$$
\boxed{\;3(k+1)^{2}+3(k+1)-12 = \left(3k^{2}+3k-12\right) + 6(k+1)\;}
$$$

The step adds a visible multiple of $ 6 $, so divisibility passes from $ k $ to $ k+1 $. Verified for every $ n $ up to $ 5000 $.

## The Proof

Write $ f(n) = 3n^{2}+3n-12 $.

> **Theorem.** For every natural number $ n $, $ 6 $ divides $ f(n) $.
>
> **Proof.** By induction on $ n $.
>
> **Basis.** At $ n=0 $, $ f(0) = -12 = 6\cdot(-2) $, and $ -2 $ is an integer, so $ 6\mid f(0) $.
>
> **Inductive step.** Let $ k $ be a particular but arbitrarily chosen natural number and suppose $ f(k) = 6m $ for some integer $ m $. Expanding,
>
> $ f(k+1) = 3k^{2}+6k+3+3k+3-12 = \left(3k^{2}+3k-12\right)+6k+6 . $
>
> So $ f(k+1) = f(k)+6(k+1) $, and substituting the hypothesis,
>
> $ f(k+1) = 6m+6(k+1) = 6\left(m+k+1\right) . $
>
> Since $ m+k+1 $ is an integer, $ 6\mid f(k+1) $.
>
> Basis and step together give the result for every $ n $. $ \blacksquare $

The basis value is negative, and that is fine: $ d\mid a $ means $ a = dq $ for some *integer* $ q $, and negative integers count. The first two values, $ f(0)=-12 $ and $ f(1)=-6 $, are both multiples of $ 6 $.

## The Picture

The values grow by $ 6, 12, 18, \ldots $, and each one is six times a number two less than a triangular number, as drawn below.

!(78%)[The step adds a multiple of 6, and the quotient is a triangular number minus 2](problem-5-steps.svg "A diagram in four parts. The top band states the step identity f of k plus one equals f of k plus six times k plus one, where f of n is three n squared plus three n minus twelve. The left panel is a bar chart of f of n for n from zero to six: minus twelve, minus six, six, twenty-four, forty-eight, seventy-eight, one hundred fourteen, with the jumps six, twelve, eighteen, twenty-four, thirty, thirty-six marked between bars. The right panel shows triangles of dots for n from two to five, with three, six, ten and fifteen dots, two of each drawn dashed as removed, leaving one, four, eight and thirteen: the quotient f of n over six. A green band notes that three n squared plus three n plus c is divisible by six for every n exactly when six divides c. An orange band notes that six is the best constant, and that twelve divides f of n exactly when n is congruent to zero or three mod four.")

## Remark — Why This Step Is Easier Than Problems 1 and 2

In 5.3 Problem 1 the step added $ 3k(k+1) $, and in Problem 2 it added $ 3\left(k^{2}+k+2\right) $. Both are obviously multiples of $ 3 $, but the sharper remarks there, that $ 6 $ divides as well, needed the extra fact that $ k(k+1) $ is even. Here the increment $ 6(k+1) $ is a multiple of $ 6 $ on its face, so the induction needs nothing else.

The reason is a general feature of quadratics. For any $ a,b $,

$$$
\left(a(k+1)^{2}+b(k+1)\right)-\left(ak^{2}+bk\right) = 2ak+(a+b) ,
$$$

checked symbolically and for $ k $ from $ -100 $ to $ 99 $. With $ a=b=3 $ the difference is $ 6k+6 $. Both terms carry a factor $ 6 $ because $ 2a = 6 $ and $ a+b = 6 $. This first difference is the discrete analogue of a derivative, the same finite differences used in 5.2: a quadratic always has linear first differences.

## Remark — The Direct Proof: Triangular Numbers

Factoring gives the result without induction:

$$$
f(n) = 3n(n+1)-12 = 6\left(\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2\right) = 6\left(T_{n}-2\right) ,
$$$

where $ T_{n} = 1+2+\cdots+n $ is the $ n $-th triangular number. $ T_{n} $ is a whole number, being a sum of integers; equivalently, one of $ n $ and $ n+1 $ is even. The identity was checked for $ n $ up to $ 5000 $. The quotients are $ -2,-1,1,4,8,13,19,\ldots $, which the right panel of the figure shows as triangles with two dots removed.

The factored form also proves the statement for every *integer* $ n $, not only natural numbers, because $ n(n+1) $ is even for negative $ n $ as well. In fact $ f(-n-1) = f(n) $: the parabola is symmetric about $ n = -\tfrac{1}{2} $, so the negative integers repeat the natural-number values. Both facts were checked for $ \lvert n\rvert $ up to $ 3000 $.

## Remark — Is $ 6 $ the Best Divisor?

Yes. Since $ f(1)=-6 $, no constant larger than $ 6 $ can divide every value, and the gcd of $ f(0),\ldots,f(199) $ is $ 6 $. As in 5.3 Problem 3, the stated divisor is already sharp.

The constant $ -12 $ plays a small role. Because $ 3n^{2}+3n $ is always a multiple of $ 6 $,

$$$
6\mid3n^{2}+3n+c \text{ for every } n \iff 6\mid c ,
$$$

and a search over $ c $ from $ -30 $ to $ 30 $ finds exactly the multiples of $ 6 $. So $ -12 $ could be swapped for $ 0 $, $ \pm6 $, $ 18 $, and so on, with the same proof.

Along a subsequence there is more. $ 12\mid f(n) $ exactly when $ T_{n}-2 $ is even, that is, when $ T_{n} $ is even. The triangular numbers $ 0,1,3,6,10,15,21,28,\ldots $ are even precisely for $ n\equiv0 $ or $ 3\pmod4 $, so $ 12 $ divides $ f(n) $ for half of all $ n $, in the repeating pattern yes, no, no, yes. This was checked to $ n=2000 $.
